---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CaptureForm from "../../components/forms/capture-form.astro";
const seo = { page_description: "Technical reference on PDF Content Streams." };
---
<Layout title="PDF Content Streams" seo={seo}>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-8 py-16 prose">
    <h1>PDF Content Streams</h1>
    <p>A Content Stream is the heart of a PDF page. It contains the actual instructions that tell a PDF viewer how to draw text, graphics, and images on the screen.</p>

    <h2>Structure of a Content Stream</h2>
    <p>Content streams are written in a specific syntax that resembles PostScript. They are almost always compressed (usually with FlateDecode) to reduce file size. (You can calculate expected sizes with our <a href="/calculators/calc-1">Estimator</a>).</p>

    <p>A typical uncompressed stream might look like this:</p>
    <pre><code>BT
/F1 12 Tf
72 712 Td
(Hello, World!) Tj
ET</code></pre>
    <p>This translates to: Begin Text (BT), set font to F1 at size 12, move to position 72,712, draw the string "Hello, World!", and End Text (ET).</p>

    <h2>Content Streams vs. Annotations</h2>
    <p>It is crucial to understand that <strong>annotations are not part of the content stream</strong>. When you use an <a href="/tools/annotate-pdf">online tool</a> to draw a highlight over text, you are not modifying the instructions shown above. Instead, you are adding an object to the <code>Annots</code> array of the page dictionary (as defined in <a href="/reference/topic-1">ISO 32000</a>), which tells the viewer to draw the highlight <em>on top</em> of the rendered content stream.</p>

    <div class="mt-12 not-prose p-6 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold mb-4">Did this technical reference help?</h3>
      <CaptureForm formLabel="feedback" submitText="Send Feedback" />
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
